{"authored" [{"question" "q-31214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,4)$ only. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,4)$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the statements are true."] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the intervals on which the function $f(x) = x^3 -3x$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3740.json" "file_sha256" "d9fbc58d96fa43f3aee64196ad54d480022dc7e7f0c143612f949dd9110bcc6e" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)= x^3 -3x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 \\\\ x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3740.json" "file_sha256" "d9fbc58d96fa43f3aee64196ad54d480022dc7e7f0c143612f949dd9110bcc6e" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3745.json" "file_sha256" "f1492a561f40618311f1c2740a4d7ebbfbde9f2fd7ee0cacdc4f537a3c749ea2" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x),$ we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3745.json" "file_sha256" "f1492a561f40618311f1c2740a4d7ebbfbde9f2fd7ee0cacdc4f537a3c749ea2" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3741.json" "file_sha256" "95244f3298536c1daf9bd85d41df8b368fbd8e7b7635f0abfbcc7ab021f8febd" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3741.json" "file_sha256" "95244f3298536c1daf9bd85d41df8b368fbd8e7b7635f0abfbcc7ab021f8febd" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/c528a8bdbb9bc6dd1a9895109fdc6818b70787c5024c78e06e2d2ec3997777d6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "cb74f35e28f48a8da60ea8063c1e95a23d10efd7e404b4e564f553c67fb6609a" "basis" 1369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$  Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "cb74f35e28f48a8da60ea8063c1e95a23d10efd7e404b4e564f553c67fb6609a" "basis" 1369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ce03f4f75f1275c5c0ab23092d155e04e8161764ce97c90f3fc6d28c8e8dede" "basis" 1369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "cb74f35e28f48a8da60ea8063c1e95a23d10efd7e404b4e564f553c67fb6609a" "basis" 1369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-\\infty ,0)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "cb74f35e28f48a8da60ea8063c1e95a23d10efd7e404b4e564f553c67fb6609a" "basis" 1369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,1)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "cb74f35e28f48a8da60ea8063c1e95a23d10efd7e404b4e564f553c67fb6609a" "basis" 1369 "verification" "/home/jake/Develope