eld" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-31221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,1)"] ["math" "(-5,0)"] ["math" "(-5,5)"] ["math" "(0,5)"] ["math" "(1,5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-31221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,5)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/9ac6bf6978169aaa3a29c570234cdbf26f37b6e91488d8e27c63255b8cbb3ba6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval  $(1,2)$ only. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(1,2)$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fe74a5f33fb1820ffabf22a6c87527c6ab012dbdae55ed8ef38b4201dc8ed8b"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(1,2)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(2,3)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,2)$ only"] ["text" "$(2,3)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(1,2)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-12 & >0\\,⟹\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $6x-12<0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fe74a5f33fb1820ffabf22a6c87527c6ab012dbdae55ed8ef38b4201dc8ed8b"} {"question" "q-79039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+(2x-2){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for all real values $x\\ne 0,$ the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ So $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{x}<0,$ which does not have solutions. So $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fe74a5f33fb1820ffabf22a6c87527c6ab012dbdae55ed8ef38b4201dc8ed8b"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fe74a5f33fb1820ffabf22a6c87527c6ab012dbdae55ed8ef38b4201dc8ed8b"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fe74a5f33fb1820ffabf22a6c87527c6ab012dbdae55ed8ef38b4201dc8ed8b"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-2x$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,2)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x=12x(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x(x-2) & >0 \\\\ x(x-2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/cb06747b13b36bc1b53f96df8d1345332200ecb8b7728342d7086963b51d86f5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-2)>0$ when $x<0$ and $x>2.$ So the graph is concave up on the intervals  $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-2)<0,$ which has the solution $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fe74a5f33fb1820ffabf22a6c87527c6ab012dbdae55ed8ef38b4201dc8ed8b"} {"question" "q-79040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "q-79040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c011939a23a6e5d0debb54f085c155a61e7ae23ef2788846231114773e7b932"} {"question" "e-3740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the intervals on which the function $f(x) = x^3 -3x$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/example-3740.json" "file_sha256" "0d039b338fcd185df2bd4acab3c818f51cc02795720168a23c5e332e7201cabc"} {"question" "e-3740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)= x^3 -3x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 \\\\ x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/example-3740.json" "file_sha256" "0d039b338fcd185df2bd4acab3c818f51cc02795720168a23c5e332e7201cabc"} {"question" "e-3745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/example-3745.json" "file_sha256" "8ec7b10267d1333dba937d5c5bc291fd0fb6cebe345fdcf3dbe456d44bcad825"} {"question" "e-3745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x),$ we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/example-3745.json" "file_sha256" "8ec7b10267d1333dba937d5c5bc291fd0fb6cebe345fdcf3dbe456d44bcad825"} {"question" "e-3741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/example-3741.json" "file_sha256" "81243d517b71ed2b15709b9ecb32a24b779de9d535ffc7dfa0921ab8f6692ee0"} {"question" "e-3741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/example-3741.json